      "text": "\nUPROPERTY(EditAnywhere, BlueprintReadWrite, Category = \"Level Progression\")\nTSubclassOf<ULevelScriptActor> NextLevel;\n\nvoid OnPlayerReachedCheckpoint()\n{\n    UGameplayStatics::LoadStreamLevel(GetWorld(), NextLevel, true, true);\n}\n```\n13. Add save and load functionality:\n* In the game instance blueprint, create variables to store the player's progress, such as current level, player stats, inventory, etc.\n* In the game instance blueprint, create functions to save the game to a file and load the game from a file.\n* In the level blueprint, call the save function when the player reaches a checkpoint or when the game is closed.\n* In the main menu or other relevant UI, add buttons to load a saved game.\n\nExample game instance blueprint code:\n```scss\nUPROPERTY(EditAnywhere, BlueprintReadWrite, Category = \"Save Game\")\nTSubclassOf<USaveGame> SaveGameClass;\n\nvoid SaveGame()\n{\n    USaveGame* SaveGameInstance = Cast<USaveGame>(UGameplayStatics::CreateSaveGameObject(SaveGameClass));\n    if (SaveGameInstance)\n    {\n        SaveGameInstance->CurrentLevel = CurrentLevel;\n        SaveGameInstance->PlayerStats = PlayerStats;\n        SaveGameInstance->Inventory = Inventory;\n        UGameplayStatics::SaveGameToSlot(SaveGameInstance, SaveGameInstance->SlotName, SaveGameInstance->UserIndex);\n    }\n}\n\nvoid LoadGame()\n{\n    USaveGame* SaveGameInstance = Cast<USaveGame>(UGameplayStatics::LoadGameFromSlot(SaveGameInstance->SlotName, SaveGameInstance->UserIndex));\n    if (SaveGameInstance)\n    {\n        CurrentLevel = SaveGameInstance->CurrentLevel;\n        PlayerStats = SaveGameInstance->PlayerStats;\n        Inventory = SaveGameInstance->Inventory;\n    }\n}\n```\n14. Add inventory and item system:\n* Create a new blueprint class for items (e.g. \"ItemBase\").\n* In the item blueprint, define properties such as name, description, type, and any other relevant information.\n* In the player character blueprint, create an inventory array to store the player's items.\n* Add functions to the player character blueprint to add, remove, and use items.\n* In the UI blueprint, create widgets to display the player's inventory and allow the player to interact with items.\n\nExample item blueprint code:\n```scss\nUPROPERTY(EditAnywhere, BlueprintReadWrite, Category = \"Item\")\nFText Name;\n\nUPROPERTY(EditAnywhere, BlueprintReadWrite, Category = \"Item\")\nFText Description;\n\nUPROPERTY(EditAnywhere, BlueprintReadWrite, Category = \"Item\")\nEItemType Type;\n\n// Example item type enum\nUENUM(BlueprintType)\nenum class EItemType : uint8\n{\n    Consumable,\n    Weapon,\n    Armor,\n    Key\n};\n```\nExample player character blueprint code:\n```scss\nUPROPERTY(EditAnywhere, BlueprintReadWrite, Category = \"Inventory\")\nTArray<UItemBase\\*> Inventory;\n\nvoid AddItem(UItemBase\\* Item)\n{\n    if (Item)\n    {\n        Inventory.Add(Item);\n    }\n}\n\nvoid RemoveItem(UItemBase\\* Item)\n{\n    if (Item)\n    {\n        Inventory.Remove(Item);\n    }\n}\n\nvoid UseItem(UItemBase\\* Item)\n{\n    if (Item)\n    {\n        // Implement item usage logic\n    }\n}\n```\n15. Add quest system:\n* Create a new blueprint class for quests (e.g. \"QuestBase\").\n* In the quest blueprint, define properties such as name, description, objectives, and rewards.\n* In the game instance blueprint, create a list of active quests and a list of completed quests.\n* Add functions to the game instance blueprint to add, complete, and update quests.\n* In the level blueprint, trigger quests when the player reaches certain areas or completes certain actions.\n* In the UI blueprint, create widgets to display the player's active quests and allow the player to interact with them.\n\nExample quest blueprint code:\n```scss\nUPROPERTY(EditAnywhere, BlueprintReadWrite, Category = \"Quest\")\nFText Name;\n\nUPROPERTY(EditAnywhere, BlueprintReadWrite, Category = \"Quest\")\nFText Description;\n\nUPROPERTY(EditAnywhere, BlueprintReadWrite, Category = \"Quest\")\n```\nOkay, let's see. The user provided a detailed guide on creating a combat system in Unreal Engine 5, similar to Genshin Impact. They've gone through steps 1 to 15, covering character setup, combat mechanics, animations, UI, sounds, particles, AI, level progression, save/load, inventory, and quests. Now, they want me to continue from where they left off. \n\nLooking at the last code block, the user was working on the quest system. The example code for the game instance blueprint includes a list of active quests and completed quests. The code snippet ends with a comment about adding functions to manage quests. The user's last message ends with \"Add",
